WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.522
A network cable is cut between two data centres. Does your bank app refuse writes or risk disagreeing balances? That choice is the CAP theorem.

00:00:12.722 --> 00:00:13.651
Pause and think.

00:00:14.051 --> 00:00:19.241
With three replicas, what write and read quorum sizes guarantee you read the latest write?

00:00:19.641 --> 00:00:26.955
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:28.155 --> 00:00:37.013
This lesson covers the CAP theorem, consistency levels, and quorums: the vocabulary behind every distributed storage interview question.

00:00:37.072 --> 00:00:52.548
New word: C A P. CAP theorem: in a network split, a distributed database must choose Consistency or Availability. Picture it like this: Two bank branches cut off by a storm: either both stop serving or they risk disagreeing.

00:00:52.948 --> 00:01:02.317
New word: quorum. The minimum number of replicas that must agree for a read or write to count. Picture it like this: A committee needing a majority present to vote.

00:01:04.822 --> 00:01:09.083
Picture two bank branches that share account balances over a phone line.

00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:13.910
One day the line is cut. A customer at branch one wants to withdraw money.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.283
Branch one can refuse until the line is back, which is consistent but not available.

00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:25.329
Or it can allow it and risk the other branch allowing it too, which is available but not consistent.

00:01:26.679 --> 00:01:31.323
CAP stands for consistency, availability and partition tolerance.

00:01:31.471 --> 00:01:42.814
New word: partition. A network split where machines can't reach each other; also a slice of data (shard). Picture it like this: A storm cutting the phone line between two branches.

00:01:44.421 --> 00:01:49.599
Consistency here means every read sees the latest write, as if there were one copy.

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:54.391
Availability means every request to a working node gets an answer.

00:01:54.471 --> 00:02:04.119
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:02:05.721 --> 00:02:13.001
A partition is a network split. Partitions will happen, so the real choice during one is consistency or availability.

00:02:14.351 --> 00:02:16.336
Quorums make the trade off adjustable.

00:02:16.786 --> 00:02:21.825
With N copies, a write waits for W acks, and a read asks R replicas.

00:02:21.855 --> 00:02:29.413
New word: replica. A copy of data on another machine. Picture it like this: A photocopy kept in another office.

00:02:31.063 --> 00:02:39.097
If R plus W is greater than N, every read set overlaps every write set, so a read always reaches at least one fresh copy.

00:02:39.547 --> 00:02:46.420
With N equals three, W equals two and R equals two, you can lose one node and still read your writes.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.417
Consistency levels from strongest to weakest.

00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:54.327
Linearizable: behaves like one copy with one clock.

00:02:54.777 --> 00:02:57.679
Read your writes: you always see your own updates.

00:02:58.129 --> 00:03:03.377
Eventual: replicas converge if writes stop, but reads can be stale for a while.

00:03:03.827 --> 00:03:08.494
PACELC adds: even without a partition, you trade latency for consistency.

00:03:08.612 --> 00:03:23.786
New word: PACELC. Extension of CAP: if Partitioned choose Availability or Consistency, Else choose Latency or Consistency. Picture it like this: Even on a sunny day, a fast answer and a perfectly checked answer trade off.

00:03:24.186 --> 00:03:32.650
New word: latency. How long one operation takes from request to answer. Picture it like this: How long you wait for your coffee.

00:03:35.162 --> 00:03:44.868
During a partition, pick consistency or availability; quorums with R plus W greater than N give overlap. Use this language in every storage design.

00:03:44.865 --> 00:03:46.119
Back to the question.

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:51.709
With three replicas, what write and read quorum sizes guarantee you read the latest write?

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:56.799
Two and two, because two plus two is more than three, so they always overlap.

00:03:57.999 --> 00:03:58.870
Quick recap.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:02.567
In a partition, choose consistency or availability.

00:04:02.967 --> 00:04:06.206
Quorums: reads plus writes greater than replicas.

00:04:06.606 --> 00:04:08.278
Know your consistency level.
